##### MF2: RESONANCE PARAMETERS                                   1325 1451  201
                                                                  1325 1451  202
- MT151    : Resonance parameters                                 1325 1451  203
                                                                  1325 1451  204
Details about the origin of the resonance parameters for this     1325 1451  205
nuclide are given above in this description. In general, both a   1325 1451  206
Resolved and an Unresolved Resonance Range are given.             1325 1451  207
                                                                  1325 1451  208
##### MF3: REACTION CROSS SECTIONS                                1325 1451  209
                                                                  1325 1451  210
All the data present in the following MT-sections have been       1325 1451  211
calculated with TALYS, possibly normalized to experimental data.  1325 1451  212
If the maximal cross section in an excitation function over the   1325 1451  213
whole energy range does not exceed 1.e-9 b, the MT-number is not  1325 1451  214
included. Cross sections lower than 1.e-20 b are assumed to have  1325 1451  215
no physical meaning and are set to zero. All MT numbers extend up 1325 1451  216
to the highest incident energy.                                   1325 1451  217
The following reaction channels/MT numbers are generally included:1325 1451  218
                                                                  1325 1451  219
- MT1      : Total cross section                                  1325 1451  220
                                                                  1325 1451  221
Calculated with the optical model over the whole energy range,    1325 1451  222
or partly reproduced using experimental data.                     1325 1451  223
                                                                  1325 1451  224
- MT2      : Elastic scattering cross section                     1325 1451  225
                                                                  1325 1451  226
Obtained by subtracting the non-elastic cross section from the    1325 1451  227
total cross section.                                              1325 1451  228
                                                                  1325 1451  229
- MT3      : Non-elastic cross section                            1325 1451  230
                                                                  1325 1451  231
Calculated with the optical model over the whole energy range.    1325 1451  232
                                                                  1325 1451  233
- MT4      : Total inelastic cross section                        1325 1451  234
                                                                  1325 1451  235
Sum of MT=51-91.                                                  1325 1451  236
                                                                  1325 1451  237
- MT5      : (n,anything) cross section                           1325 1451  238
                                                                  1325 1451  239
MT5 contains the reactions which can not be stored in any other   1325 1451  240
MT-number. The information of MF3/MT5 can be combined with        1325 1451  241
MF6/MT5 to obtain residual production cross sections particle     1325 1451  242
production cross sections and (double-)differential cross         1325 1451  243
sections.                                                         1325 1451  244
                                                                  1325 1451  245
- MT16     : (n,2n) cross section                                 1325 1451  246
- MT17     : (n,3n) cross section                                 1325 1451  247
- MT22     : (n,na) cross section                                 1325 1451  248
- MT28     : (n,np) cross section                                 1325 1451  249
- MT51-90  : (n,n') cross section for 1st-40th excited state      1325 1451  250
- MT91     : (n,n') continuum cross section                       1325 1451  251
- MT102    : (n,gamma) cross section                              1325 1451  252
- MT103    : (n,p) cross section                                  1325 1451  253
- MT104    : (n,d) cross section                                  1325 1451  254
- MT105    : (n,t) cross section                                  1325 1451  255
- MT106    : (n,h) cross section                                  1325 1451  256
- MT107    : (n,a) cross section                                  1325 1451  257
- MT600-610: (n,p) cross section for 0th-10th excited state       1325 1451  258
- MT649    : (n,p) continuum cross section                        1325 1451  259
- MT650-655: (n,d) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        1325 1451  260
- MT699    : (n,d) continuum cross section                        1325 1451  261
- MT700-705: (n,t) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        1325 1451  262
- MT749    : (n,t) continuum cross section                        1325 1451  263
- MT750-755: (n,h) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        1325 1451  264
- MT799    : (n,h) continuum cross section                        1325 1451  265
- MT800-810: (n,a) cross section for 0th-10th excited state       1325 1451  266
- MT849    : (n,a) continuum cross section                        1325 1451  267
                                                                  1325 1451  268
##### MF4: ANGULAR DISTRIBUTIONS OF SECONDARY PARTICLES           1325 1451  269
                                                                  1325 1451  270
We use MF4 for the neutron scattering angular distribution for    1325 1451  271
discrete levels.                                                  1325 1451  272
                                                                  1325 1451  273
- MT2      : Elastic angular distribution                         1325 1451  274
                                                                  1325 1451  275
The flag LTT=3 is used to indicate a switch at some high energy,  1325 1451  276
generally 50 MeV, from a Legendre representation to a tabulated   1325 1451  277
representation. For incident energies below this energy, the      1325 1451  278
Legendre coefficients are given on a sufficiently precise energy  1325 1451  279
grid. They are a sum of calculated Legendre coefficients for      1325 1451  280
compound nucleus and shape-elastic scattering. For higher         1325 1451  281
incident energies, relative angular distributions are tabulated   1325 1451  282
on an angular grid.                                               1325 1451  283
                                                                  1325 1451  284
- MT51-70  : (n,n') angular distribution for 1st-20th state       1325 1451  285
- MT600-610: (n,p) angular distribution for 0th-10th state        1325 1451  286
- MT650-655: (n,d) angular distribution for 0th-5th state         1325 1451  287
- MT700-705: (n,t) angular distribution for 0th-5th state         1325 1451  288
- MT750-755: (n,h) angular distribution for 0th-5th state         1325 1451  289
- MT800-810: (n,a) angular distribution for 0th-10th state        1325 1451  290
                                                                  1325 1451  291
##### MF6: PRODUCT ENERGY-ANGLE DISTRIBUTIONS                     1325 1451  292
                                                                  1325 1451  293
In MF6 we store all secondary energy, angle, and energy-angle     1325 1451  294
distributions, as well as all residual and discrete + continuum   1325 1451  295
photon production cross sections for channels other than capture  1325 1451  296
(MT102) and reactions to discrete levels (MT51,etc.).             1325 1451  297
All data are generated with TALYS.                                1325 1451  298
                                                                  1325 1451  299
- MT5      : (n,anything) yields and energy-angle distributions   1325 1451  300
                                                                  1325 1451  301
MT5 contains, in this order,                                      1325 1451  302
For neutrons up to alpha particles and photons:                   1325 1451  303
- the production yields per particle for the whole energy range   1325 1451  304
- the secondary energy-angle distributions per particles which are1325 1451  305
  not represented by explicit MT numbers. On a secondary energy   1325 1451  306
  grid the relative emission spectra are given together with the  1325 1451  307
  parameters for the Kalbach systematics for angular              1325 1451  308
  distributions. Inelastic scattering cross sections for discrete 1325 1451  309
  states have been broadened and added to the continuum spectra.  1325 1451  310
For each residual product:                                        1325 1451  311
- the production yields for the whole energy range.               1325 1451  312
- the recoil                                                      1325 1451  313
These distributions can be multiplied with MF3/MT5 to get         1325 1451  314
the production cross sections and (double-)differential cross     1325 1451  315
sections.                                                         1325 1451  316
                                                                  1325 1451  317
- MT16     : (n,2n) energy-angle distr. and photon production     1325 1451  318
                                                                  1325 1451  319
First, for each type of outgoing particle, the (trivial) integer  1325 1451  320
particle yields are given. Next, on a sufficiently dense incident 1325 1451  321
energy grid the secondary energy-angle distributions are          1325 1451  322
specified by means of the relative emission spectra and the       1325 1451  323
parameters for the Kalbach systematics for angular distributions. 1325 1451  324
Next, the photon yield is tabulated as a function of incident     1325 1451  325
energy. For each incident energy, the photon production is given  1325 1451  326
for all discrete gamma lines present in the final nucleus. A      1325 1451  327
continuum photon distribution is added to this. We assume         1325 1451  328
isotropy for all produced gamma rays.                             1325 1451  329
                                                                  1325 1451  330
For the following MT-numbers, the same procedure as for MT16 is   1325 1451  331
followed:                                                         1325 1451  332
                                                                  1325 1451  333
- MT17 : (n,3n) energy-angle distr. and photon production         1325 1451  334
- MT22 : (n,na) energy-angle distr. and photon production         1325 1451  335
- MT28 : (n,np) energy-angle distr. and photon production         1325 1451  336
- MT91 : (n,n') continuum energy-angle distr. and phot. prod.     1325 1451  337
- MT649: (n,p) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     1325 1451  338
- MT699: (n,d) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     1325 1451  339
- MT749: (n,t) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     1325 1451  340
- MT809: (n,h) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     1325 1451  341
- MT849: (n,a) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     1325 1451  342
                                                                  1325 1451  343
##### MF8: RADIOACTIVE DECAY DATA                                 1325 1451  344
                                                                  1325 1451  345
For reactions to isomers, MF8 designates where the information    1325 1451  346
for isomeric versus ground state production, or residual          1325 1451  347
production can be found, i.e. for each MT number it points to     1325 1451  348
either MF6, MF9 or MF10.                                          1325 1451  349
                                                                  1325 1451  350
##### MF9: MULTIPLICITIES FOR PRODUCTION OF RADIOACTIVE NUCLIDES  1325 1451  351
                                                                  1325 1451  352
MF9 is used for isomer production by non-threshold reactions.     1325 1451  353
Final states with a lifetime that exceeds 1 second are treated    1325 1451  354
as isomer.                                                        1325 1451  355
                                                                  1325 1451  356
##### MF10: CROSS SECTIONS FOR PRODUCTION OF RADIOACTIVE NUCLIDES 1325 1451  357
                                                                  1325 1451  358
MF10 is used for isomer production by threshold reactions.        1325 1451  359
Final states with a lifetime that exceeds 1 second are treated    1325 1451  360
as isomer.                                                        1325 1451  361
                                                                  1325 1451  362
##### MF12: PHOTON PRODUCTION YIELD DATA                          1325 1451  363
                                                                  1325 1451  364
MF12 is used for the photon production for two types of reactions:1325 1451  365
- Reactions to discrete levels (MT51, etc.) For this transition   1325 1451  366
  probabilities are used (LO=2)                                   1325 1451  367
- Photons from neutron capture (MT102). Here multiplicities are   1325 1451  368
  given                                                           1325 1451  369
                                                                  1325 1451  370
##### MF14: PHOTON ANGULAR DISTRIBUTIONS                          1325 1451  371
                                                                  1325 1451  372
All photon production is considered as isotropic                  1325 1451  373
                                                                  1325 1451  374
##### MF15: PHOTON ENERGY SPECTRA                                 1325 1451  375
                                                                  1325 1451  376
For capture (MT102), the photon energy spectrum is given.         1325 1451  377
                                                                  1325 1451  378
##### MF32: COVARIANCES OF RESONANCE PARAMETERS                   1325 1451  379
                                                                  1325 1451  380
In MF32, we store the covariances of resonance parameters,        1325 1451  381
and more details are given above in this description. In most     1325 1451  382
versions of TENDL, this information is now stored in MF33.        1325 1451  383
                                                                  1325 1451  384
##### MF33: COVARIANCES OF NEUTRON CROSS SECTIONS                 1325 1451  385
                                                                  1325 1451  386
In MF33, we store the covariances of most, but not all, reaction  1325 1451  387
channels that are given in MF3. All covariances are generated     1325 1451  388
with the TASMAN code [kon19], which generates TALYS runs using    1325 1451  389
a Monte Carlo parameter method.                                   1325 1451  390
For each MT-number, the information consists of:                  1325 1451  391
1. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTa, Ea (variances)                1325 1451  392
2. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTa, Eb (correlations for the      1325 1451  393
   same reaction channel between different energies)              1325 1451  394
3. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTb, Ea (correlations between      1325 1451  395
   different reaction channels for the same energy)               1325 1451  396
4. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTb, Eb (correlations between      1325 1451  397
   different reaction channels for different energies)            1325 1451  398
Classes 1 and 2 are covered by NI-type subsubsections with        1325 1451  399
flag LB=5, followed by a LB=8 section which contains              1325 1451  400
the cross section variances multiplied by 1.e-3. Next, classes 3  1325 1451  401
and 4 are covered by flag LB=6 to account for the different       1325 1451  402
energy grids that two correlating MT-sections may have. Since     1325 1451  403
the total correlation matrix is fully symmetric, we only need to  1325 1451  404
include the correlation elements between different MT-numbers     1325 1451  405
once, and we do that in the subsection with the lowest of the     1325 1451  406
two MT-numbers. We include covariance information for all the     1325 1451  407
total cross sections, the lumped partial cross sections and the   1325 1451  408
individual inelastic cross sections per level (MT51,...). We do   1325 1451  409
not include covariances for MT600 and higher MT-numbers. For the  1325 1451  410
most important channels, we include cross-channel correlations.   1325 1451  411
                                                                  1325 1451  412
##### MF34: COVARIANCES OF ANGULAR DISTRIBUTIONS                  1325 1451  413
                                                                  1325 1451  414
In MF34, we store the covariances of the elastic scattering       1325 1451  415
angular distributions that are given in MF4/MT2. All covariances  1325 1451  416
are generated with the TASMAN code [kon19], which generates       1325 1451  417
TALYS runs using a Monte Carlo parameter method. The tables are   1325 1451  418
restricted to MT2 and contain covariances for the first 6         1325 1451  419
Legendre coefficients. Both intra- and inter-correlations of the  1325 1451  420
various Legendre coefficients are given. As in MF33, the LB5      1325 1451  421
representation is used.                                           1325 1451  422
                                                                  1325 1451  423
##### MF40: COVARIANCES OF ISOMER PRODUCTION                      1325 1451  424
                                                                  1325 1451  425
For covariances of ground state and isomeric cross sections, the  1325 1451  426
same procedures as for MF33 are used.                             1325 1451  427
                                                                  1325 1451  428
